# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What gets committed, what never leaves this machine.
# Rule of thumb: SOURCE goes in, ARTIFACTS and SECRETS stay out.
#   committed .... src/, lang/, docs/, assets/, tools/, .github/, csproj, README,
#                  CHANGELOG, LICENSE, AGENTS.md, FodyWeavers.*, App.config
#   never ........ build output, runtime state, logs, signing keys, packaged
#                  exes / zips / checksums (they are rebuilt from a tag)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Build output
bin/
obj/

# IDE / editor
.vs/
.vscode/
.claude/
.zcode/
*.user
*.suo

# Local reverse-engineering tooling config (Ghidra MCP)
.mcp_d.json

# Microsoft Taskmgr symbols for reverse engineering (~21 MB, not redistributable)
symbols/

# Windows shell cruft
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini

# Runtime artifacts (created next to the exe - normally inside bin/, guarded here
# in case the exe is ever run from the repo root)
/logs/
settings.yaml
settings.yaml.tmp
settings.yaml.bad
shutdown.sentinel
/history/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing material - PRIVATE. A leaked .pfx/.snk lets anyone ship malware as
# TaskMonitor. The CI path passes the certificate through a base64 secret;
# nothing is ever read from a committed file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.pfx
*.p12
*.snk
*.pvk
/certs/
/.signing/
/secrets/
*.pfx.b64

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Release artifacts - produced by tools/pack.ps1 from a clean tree, so they are
# rebuilt on every release and never checked in.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/dist/
release-notes.md
/publish/
task_monitor-*-x64.exe
task_monitor-*-x64.zip
task_monitor-*-x64.exe.sha256
task_monitor-*-x64.signing.txt

# Local scratch (screenshots, probe output)
/_probe/
*.tmp